type OrderEvent =
  | { type: 'OrderPlaced';              customerId: string; contractTier: string; lineItems: LineItem[]; placedAt: string }
  | { type: 'ReviewAssigned';           reviewerId: string; assignedAt: string }
  | { type: 'OrderApproved';            approvedBy: string; approvalNotes: string; approvedAt: string }
  | { type: 'OrderApprovalOverridden';  overriddenBy: string; reasonCode: string; urgency: boolean; overriddenAt: string }
  | { type: 'OrderRejected';            rejectedBy: string; rejectionReason: string; rejectedAt: string }
  | { type: 'LineItemQuantityAdjusted'; itemId: string; previousQty: number; newQty: number; adjustedBy: string; reason: string; adjustedAt: string }
  | { type: 'InvoiceGenerated';         invoiceId: string; amount: number; generatedAt: string }
  | { type: 'PaymentRecorded';          paymentRef: string; amount: number; paymentMethod: string; recordedAt: string };

Key decisions:
- OrderApproved and OrderApprovalOverridden are DISTINCT event types, not one type with an optional 'overrideBy' field. This is the structural choice that makes 'query all management overrides' a simple filter by event type rather than a scan of payload fields.
- LineItemQuantityAdjusted preserves previousQty so the event is self-contained — no need to load prior state to understand what changed.
- Each event includes its own timestamp (approvedAt, rejectedAt etc.) rather than relying on a shared 'occurred_at' — this allows events to record the precise business time, which may differ from the database write time.

